%! Values of different shapes.
%!
%! A predicate can hold values of different shapes. A pattern only
%! matches values of its own shape: (?x, ?y) matches pairs but not
%! triples, and {a = ?x} matches only maps whose single key is a. The
%! other values are skipped without an error. Accessors such as ?v[2]
%! and GET(?v, b) can be applied to any value. If the value has no such
%! part, the rule derives nothing for it, and Nemo counts these cases in
%! a warning.
%!
%! The example shows:
%! - how patterns select values of one shape,
%! - how accessors treat values without the requested part,
%! - how isTuple and isMap check the kind of a value.

value((1, 2)) .
value((1, 2, 3)) .
value({a = 1}) .
value({a = 1, b = 2}) .
value(plain) .

pair(?x, ?y) :- value((?x, ?y)) .
onlyKeyA(?x) :- value({a = ?x}) .
third(?v, ?z) :- value(?v), ?z = ?v[2] .
keyB(?v, ?z) :- value(?v), ?z = GET(?v, b) .
tuple(?v) :- value(?v), isTuple(?v) .
map(?v) :- value(?v), isMap(?v) .

@output pair, onlyKeyA, third, keyB, tuple, map .
